<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ayyubid Emirate of Kerak</h1><p class="st-meta">1229 CE – 1263 CE · capital Kerak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ayyubid principality based on the Crusader castle of Kerak, which held out until 1263, after most of Ayyubid Syria had fallen.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-dawud">Al-Nasir Dawud</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muazzam-isa">al-Mu'azzam Isa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, lost Damascus to his uncles in 1229 but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>, which became a separate principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-dawud">Al-Nasir Dawud</a> was a poet and scholar who used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> as a base for intrigue, briefly occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1239 and holding, then releasing and backing, the future sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/as-salih-ayyub">al-Salih Ayyub</a>. The principality controlled the routes from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hijaz</a>, and the near-impregnable castle made it a refuge. From 1250 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mughith-umar">al-Mughith Umar</a> ruled Kerak, harbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> dissidents and challenging the new regime in Egypt.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1263 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> lured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mughith-umar">al-Mughith Umar</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a>, arrested and executed him, and annexed the principality.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1229 CE – 1263 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.16%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1229 CE – 1263 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kerak</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>al-Nasir Dawud, al-Mughith Umar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ayyubids of Kerak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1246&amp;focus=ayyubid-emirate-of-kerak" aria-label="Ayyubid Emirate of Kerak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5618 1058 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ayyubid Emirate of Kerak lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5815 1335h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5938 1314h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5938 1314h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Jordan. <a href="/atlas?year=1246&amp;focus=ayyubid-emirate-of-kerak">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

